  that to slow you down; it's a design input, and it's much cheaper to decide now than to retrofit.

  First question, the one everything else hangs off:

  [1mThe fork:[0m "the actual user" needs an authoritative source, and this app has none — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub with no backend,
  and the only user-supplied data is a typed username and password. Where the id originates determines what you can
  store, what it's worth, and what a later real backend does to it.

  - [1mServer-issued after auth[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns a [38;5;153muserId[39m; we persist it and later forms read it. This is the only option
    that genuinely identifies the account, and it's what a real backend will do anyway. Cost: the contract doesn't exist
    yet, so today it's a stub you'd swap out. Nothing pre-login has an id.
  - [1mAnonymous local id, upgraded at login[0m — mint a UUID on first visit, persist it, and link it to the server's [38;5;153muserId[39m
    once auth succeeds. Buys continuity across forms filled [3mbefore[0m login, plus a clean join key. Cost: two ids and a
    linking step — the most moving parts of the three.
  - [1mLocal-only persistent id[0m — a UUID in storage, no backend involvement. Works today with no contract. Cost: it
    identifies a browser, not a person — it breaks across devices and survives a user switch, which is exactly what you
    said you didn't want.

  [1mMy recommendation: server-issued, stubbed today.[0m You said "actual user," and that rules out the local-only id on its
  own terms. The hybrid is the right answer [3mif[0m forms get filled before anyone logs in — but that's a real cost in
  complexity, so I'd rather you tell me it's needed than assume it.
